From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 01:24:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 86/86] web-app: "what's new" device message per release
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

For most users the device message is the only release note they will ever
read, and nothing shipped one: updateDeviceChat() only replayed upstream
Delta Chat's 2.3.0 changelog entry, so a SlothfulChat release told existing
accounts nothing at all.

Add a SLOTHFUL_RELEASE_MESSAGES table appended to updateDeviceChat(), one
entry per release, oldest first. A table rather than a patch per release:
later releases amend this one patch instead of growing the stack, and the
existing `changelog-version-<v>` label convention already makes core drop
repeats, so re-runs are free and an account only gets the versions it has
not seen yet. Fresh accounts still skip the lot via skipCurrentChangelog.

Drop the inherited `changelog-version-2.3.0` message while we are here. It
describes what Delta Chat 2.0 brought over 1.x; SlothfulChat's first release
was already past that, so it announced an upgrade none of our accounts ever
lived through. That was the only caller of `tx` in this function.

Text is a plain literal, not an _untranslated_en.json key — release blurbs
go stale long before anyone could translate them. It closes on the version's
own changelog anchor (#v-0.9.0), which survives later releases where a
positional link would not.

Seeded with 0.9.0.
---
 packages/frontend/src/deviceMessages.ts | 41 ++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 37 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/packages/frontend/src/deviceMessages.ts b/packages/frontend/src/deviceMessages.ts
index 3b180fa..6ca3a20 100644
--- a/packages/frontend/src/deviceMessages.ts
+++ b/packages/frontend/src/deviceMessages.ts
@@ -6,7 +6,6 @@ export async function updateDeviceChat(
   accountId: number,
   skipCurrentChangelog: boolean = false
 ) {
-  const tx = window.static_translate
   const addDeviceMessage = async (
     label: string,
     msg: Partial<Parameters<typeof BackendRemote.rpc.addDeviceMessage>[2]>
@@ -28,11 +27,45 @@ export async function updateDeviceChat(
       })
     }
   }
-  await addDeviceMessage(`changelog-version-2.3.0`, {
-    text: tx('update_2_0', 'https://delta.chat/donate'),
-  })
+  // Upstream's `changelog-version-2.3.0` entry is deliberately gone: it
+  // announces what Delta Chat 2.0 brought over 1.x, and no SlothfulChat
+  // release ever predates that, so every account here would be told about
+  // an upgrade it never lived through.
+  for (const [version, text] of SLOTHFUL_RELEASE_MESSAGES) {
+    await addDeviceMessage(`changelog-version-${version}`, { text })
+  }
 }
 
+/**
+ * One "what's new" message per SlothfulChat release, oldest first. For most
+ * users this is the only release note they ever read, so it is the release's
+ * user-facing summary rather than a changelog dump — see RELEASING.md, which
+ * also requires a human to approve the text before it ships.
+ *
+ * Appending here (rather than one patch per release) keeps the whole history
+ * in a single patch that later releases amend. The `changelog-version-` label
+ * makes core drop every repeat, so re-runs are free and existing accounts get
+ * only the versions they have not seen.
+ *
+ * Plain text, not markdown: device messages render literally and URLs
+ * autolink. The trailing link uses the version's own changelog anchor, so it
+ * keeps pointing at this release once later ones ship.
+ */
+const SLOTHFUL_RELEASE_MESSAGES: ReadonlyArray<readonly [string, string]> = [
+  [
+    '0.9.0',
+    `SlothfulChat 0.9.0
+
+• Voice messages have a real player: seek on the waveform, change speed, and keep listening while you switch chats.
+• Big attachments show their type, size and a preview before you download them, with a live percentage — and an interrupted download resumes instead of restarting.
+• Longer messages can be expanded in the chat to read them in full, and you can copy the text of several messages at once.
+• Faster: adding a profile is near-instant, and encryption no longer stalls the app.
+• Privacy and security: exported backups no longer linger in browser storage, no usage statistics leave before you have seen the opt-out notice, and answering a call no longer starts your camera by itself.
+
+https://web.slothful.chat/changelog/?p=web-app#v-0.9.0`,
+  ],
+]
+
 /**
